# ── Local Convex CLI pointing at SELF-HOSTED production backend (Azure App Service) ──
# Run with: pnpm dev:prod (loads this file's local copy via dotenv -e .env.production-local)
#
# Production self-hosts Convex on Azure App Service — see infra/convex-production.bicep
# and .github/workflows/deploy-convex-production.yml — so the "SELF-HOSTED" block below
# is the ACTIVE one. Production has never run on Convex Cloud, and neither has any other
# environment in this project (`develop` isn't a deployed environment at all — it's
# local-only, same as .env.example). The "CONVEX CLOUD" block below stays empty on
# purpose: the Convex CLI refuses to run when a Cloud deployment ref is set alongside
# self-hosted vars, so these three vars exist solely to keep the CLI happy, not because
# anything reads them.

# =============================================================================
# CONVEX CLOUD   ← INACTIVE (production self-hosts; leave empty)
# =============================================================================
CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY=
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=
CONVEX_CLOUD_URL=

# =============================================================================
# SELF-HOSTED    ← ACTIVE for production
# =============================================================================
# CLI targeting of the production self-hosted backend on Azure App Service.
# URL is the Web App's default hostname (infra/convex-production.bicep ->
# modules/convex-app-service.bicep); ADMIN_KEY is the signed token generated
# once via generate_admin_key.sh and stored as the CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_ADMIN_KEY
# GitHub secret (see docs/deployment/convex-production-runbook.md#a4-generate-the-instance-secret-postgres-url-and-admin-key).
# Treat this admin key like a production credential — it grants full read/write
# access to the production Convex deployment.
CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_URL=https://retrotool-prod-convex.azurewebsites.net
CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_ADMIN_KEY=replace-with-admin-key-from-generate_admin_key.sh

# The container runtime variables that .env.example sets for local Docker
# (CONVEX_INSTANCE_NAME / CONVEX_INSTANCE_SECRET / CONVEX_POSTGRES_URL /
# CONVEX_DO_NOT_REQUIRE_SSL) do NOT apply here — production isn't run via
# docker-compose. Those equivalents (INSTANCE_NAME, INSTANCE_SECRET,
# POSTGRES_URL, DO_NOT_REQUIRE_SSL) are set directly on the App Service by
# infra/modules/convex-app-service.bicep, with the two secrets resolved from
# Key Vault (retrotool-prod-cvx-<hash>) at runtime — never set them here.
# For reference: INSTANCE_NAME is retrotool-convex-prod, which Convex derives
# into the Postgres database name retrotool_convex_prod (hyphens -> underscores).

# =============================================================================
# SHARED — API auth integration (applies regardless of Cloud vs self-hosted)
# =============================================================================
CONVEX_BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://retro-tool-api.azurewebsites.net/api/auth

# ── JWT verification (set ON THE PRODUCTION CONVEX DEPLOYMENT via the CLI) ───
# auth.config.ts reads these via process.env inside Convex functions, so set them
# on the deployment — NOT here (this file only configures the CLI). Verify
# deployed values with `convex env list` (using CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_URL/_ADMIN_KEY
# above). deploy-convex-production.yml sets these automatically on every deploy
# ("Configure Convex auth and deploy functions" step), so this is only needed for
# a fresh bring-up outside that workflow.
JWT_ISSUER=https://retro-tool-api.azurewebsites.net
JWT_AUDIENCE=convex
JWT_JWKS_URL=https://retro-tool-api.azurewebsites.net/api/auth/jwks

# ── NOTE on the production API hostname ───────────────────────────────────────
# Production's API App Service predates the Bicep naming convention and keeps
# its legacy name `retro-tool-api` (not `retrotool-prod-api`) — see the legacy
# naming callout in docs/infra/provisioning.md. JWT_ISSUER must byte-match the
# deployed API's BETTER_AUTH_URL exactly (scheme / host / trailing slash) or
# every Convex query throws Unauthenticated.
